{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JcZeQNr4bXgx4kk44YbksB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experiment data for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment_plan, under the \"median\" tier assumptions. Each prediction needs a result_rationale explaining why this outcome is expected under the median tier.\n\nLet me carefully go through each planned experiment and generate realistic data consistent with the median tier definition.\n\nKey median tier parameters:\n- acr_vla_libero_plus_success_gain_abs: [6, 11]\n- a3_ablation_gap_no_conformal_vs_full: [3, 7]\n- a4_ablation_gap_scalar_vs_perDOF: [1, 3]\n\n\n- coc_success_spearman_rho: [0.3, 0.5]\n- aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: [10, 20]\n- srpo_stacking_gain_abs: [2, 4]\n- srpo_stacked_absolute: [84, 86]\n- libero_nominal_drop: [1, 3]\n- perturbation_dims_with_significant_gain: [3, 5]\n\nI'm establishing the baseline assumptions—SmolVLA on LIBERO-Plus should perform around 68% success rate as a typical starting point for a VLA with perturbations applied.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific experimental results. SRPO achieves 82.1% on LIBERO-Plus, while the base VLA hits about 95% on unperturbed LIBERO tasks. For plan_001, the ACR-VLA pipeline shows a modest 2% drop on nominal LIBERO but gains roughly 8% on the perturbed L3-L5 subset. Plan_002 is the critical ablation—removing conformal features should create a 3-7% performance gap, putting the non-conformal variant around 71%. I'm continuing through the remaining ablations to map out how each component contributes. uncertainty\n- Gap [1,3] between scalar and per-DOF\n- Scalar: ~74% (vs 76% full), gap ~2%\n\n**plan_004**: SRPO stacking\n- SRPO base: 82.1%\n- Stacked: ~85% (within [84,86])\n- Gain: ~3% (within [2,4])\n\n**plan_005**: COC signal validation\n- Spearman ρ: ~0.38 (within [0.3, 0.5])\n- ACI convergence: converges but slowly\n- Ellipsoid narrowing: ~15% (within [10,20])\n\n**plan_006**: A1 - CRM without ACI (static calibration)\n- Without ACI, the conformal radii are static. Performance would drop somewhat.\n- Maybe ~73% on LIBERO-Plus (vs 76% full), so ACI contributes ~3%\n\n**plan_007**: A2 - ACI without CRM\n- Using just CGS and COC for sample selection without the conformal radius mechanism drops performance to around 72%, suggesting CRM adds roughly 4% on top of the base approach.\n\n**plan_008**: Per-perturbation breakdown\n- Looking at how each of the 7 perturbation dimensions affects performance, I see that camera, noise, robot-state, and light show meaningful gains of 12%, 10%, 8%, and 5% respectively, while background, layout, and language contribute minimally.\n\n**plan_009**: A5 - CGS joint Mahalanobis vs per-DOF Bonferroni\n- The joint Mahalanobis approach should edge out the per-DOF Bonferroni correction, expecting around 76% versus 74.5%.\n\n**plan_010**: A6 - COC vs episode-level success for ACI\n- COC provides a weaker but step-level signal, while episode-level success is stronger but sparser; episode-level ACI reaches about 77% compared to COC's 76%, suggesting episode-level is slightly more effective even though COC shows promise.\n\n**plan_011**: A7 - DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic\n- Aleatoric uncertainty alone gets 73%, epistemic uncertainty reaches 76%, and combining both features hits 76.5%, confirming that epistemic uncertainty is the more valuable signal under distribution shift.\n\n**plan_012**: Feature importance analysis\n- Gradient-based analysis reveals that per-DOF standard deviation features dominate for camera, noise, and robot-state perturbations, while conformal radius features matter more for robot-state robustness.\n\n**plan_013**: SnapFlow integration\n- K=10 with 1-step inference delivers roughly 3x speedup while maintaining about 85% sample diversity, though performance dips slightly to 75%.\n\nNow I'm working through the YAML output structure and need to ensure each planned experiment has detailed data, clear rationales, and proper descriptions of tables and figures. Looking back at the LIBERO-Plus baseline, SRPO achieves 82.1% as the current state-of-the-art, so I need to frame how the base SmolVLA compares and what improvements we're targeting.\n\nFor the baseline, non-robust VLAs typically perform around 60-70% on LIBERO-Plus. I'll estimate SmolVLA at 65% on the full benchmark, which gives ACR-VLA roughly 73% with the expected 6-11% improvement range. Since the experiment focuses on the harder L3-L5 perturbation levels, the baseline there would be lower around 55-60%, but I'll work with the aggregate success rate for consistency.\n\nOn standard LIBERO nominal tasks, the base model sits around 95%, and ACR-VLA drops slightly to 93%—a 2% decrease that fits within the expected 1-3% range. Now I'm working through the ablations to validate the contribution breakdown: full ACR-VLA at 73%, removing conformal features drops it to 68% (5% gap), and scalar-only drops to 71% (2% gap). But this creates a tension—if the base is 65% and A3 without conformal features is 68%, that's only a 3% gain from any uncertainty signal, leaving 5% for conformal features alone. That doesn't align with how the improvements should decompose. Most gain comes from having any uncertainty signal vs none. The A3 ablation shows observation-conditioned residual alone gives +3%, adding scalar uncertainty gives another +3%, and upgrading to per-DOF uncertainty adds +2% more. So the total uncertainty contribution is +5% compared to just the residual, which suggests the narrative should emphasize that calibrated uncertainty features provide substantial gains beyond the base correction mechanism. of +3%. So the narrative shifts—it's not that per-DOF is the dominant factor, but rather that structured uncertainty conditioning as a whole drives improvements, with per-DOF contributing incrementally on top.\n\nThis aligns with the median results, so I'll move forward. Looking at SRPO stacking, adding ACR-VLA yields a 2.9% gain, which fits the expected range. For the COC metrics, the Spearman correlation sits at 0.38, convergence happens around 150 episodes, and ellipsoid volume contracts by 15%—all within reasonable bounds. Breaking down the per-perturbation ablations, camera robustness shows the strongest gain at 12%, followed by noise at 10% and robot-state at 9%, while lighting and background contribute more modestly.\n\nNow I need to construct the full YAML with comprehensive tables and analysis across all experiments. I'm reconsidering the LIBERO-Plus performance structure—the task mentions nominal preservation on LIBERO paired with robustness gains on the harder L3-L5 levels. I should provide per-level breakdowns for the main pipeline experiment, starting with aggregate performance across all five perturbation levels.\n\nLooking at the per-level results, the gains increase substantially at higher difficulty levels, with L3-L5 showing the most improvement. But I'm realizing the evaluation should focus specifically on L3-L5 as the robustness metric rather than averaging across all levels.\n\nLet me recalibrate the baseline numbers. SRPO hits 82.1% on LIBERO-Plus, which I'll treat as the full benchmark performance. For SmolVLA without robustness methods, I'm estimating around 62%, which gives us roughly 8% gain with ACR-VLA to reach 70%. Stacking SRPO and ACR-VLA together pushes performance to 85%. On the nominal LIBERO task, the base is around 95% but ACR-VLA causes a slight 2% drop, likely due to the robustness training trade-off.\n\nNow I have enough to construct a detailed YAML with comprehensive breakdowns and per-task metrics.\n\nFor the tables, I'll embed markdown-style representations directly in the YAML strings. Let me work through the specific experimental results: plan_005 shows the COC signal validation with a Spearman correlation of 0.38 between episode coverage and success, the ACI convergence stabilizes after roughly 150 episodes with some initial overshoot, and the ellipsoid volume narrows by about 15% compared to static calibration at matched coverage levels. For plan_006 without ACI, static thresholds remain fixed from the calibration set, which causes miscalibration under distribution shift and results in roughly 70% performance versus 73% with the full method—so ACI contributes about 3 percentage points. Plan_007 begins exploring the A2 variant but appears to cut off mid-thought.\n\nContinuing with the ablations: plan_009 compares joint Mahalanobis against Bonferroni correction, where joint achieves 73% (matching the full ACR-VLA) while Bonferroni's conservative approach yields 71.5%, a gap of about 1.5 percentage points. Plan_010 contrasts COC-based ACI at 73% against episode-success-based ACI at 74%, suggesting the oracle episode signal is slightly stronger than the COC proxy. Plan_011 examines uncertainty quantification choices, comparing DiU aleatoric features at 70% against CGS epistemic features at 73%.\n\nNow looking at the combined feature set and validation results. The 52D combined approach reaches 73.5%, confirming that epistemic uncertainty outperforms aleatoric under distribution shift. For feature importance, gradient analysis shows per-DOF standard deviation features matter most for camera and noise dimensions, while conformal radius is critical for robot state and ellipsoid volume contributes modestly. Plan_013 shows SnapFlow achieves a 3x inference speedup (0.12s down to 0.04s) with a slight performance trade-off to 72%, maintaining reasonable diversity at a covariance trace ratio of 0.82.\n\nNow I need to structure the results into proper tables and figures for the paper, starting with the full pipeline baseline on LIBERO nominal conditions.\n\nWriting YAML configuration...\n\nNow I'm establishing baseline performance numbers to ensure the results tell a consistent story—SmolVLA achieves 94.8% on nominal LIBERO tasks but drops to 62.3% on LIBERO-Plus, with ACR-VLA improving that aggregate to 70.5% while maintaining comparable nominal performance.\n\nLooking at the breakdown across robustness dimensions, ACR-VLA shows the strongest gains on camera shift and sensor noise (12.6% and 10.8% respectively), moderate improvements on robot state and lighting, and minimal gains on layout and language tasks. The ablations confirm that conformal prediction features account for most of the improvement, with scalar uncertainty alone closing about 2% of the gap to the full pipeline. Looking at the remaining ablation results—A7 shows that the combined approach with 52 dimensions outperforms both individual uncertainty methods, SnapFlow achieves competitive performance with significantly faster inference, and the numbers all check out against the expected ranges for the gains and drops. Now I'm ready to write up the YAML configuration. within range. Good.\n\nAll the validation checks pass, so I can move forward with writing the output. I need to structure the YAML to include the 13 planned experiments (plan_001 through plan_013), since plan_000 is already marked as done. I'll format the experiment data as markdown tables within YAML multiline strings.\n\nGenerating YAML output...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11182,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11182},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef3a4a7f-e7d7-4354-91e3-42aa53e20677","uuid":"01fddd35-b619-4cd0-b732-cb978b2bc68e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JcZeQNr4bXgx4kk44YbksB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated relative to synthetic MVP. Story pivots from \"per-DOF is the key innovation\"\n  to \"structured uncertainty conditioning (including per-DOF) significantly improves inference-time correction.\"\n  Ablation decomposition: CRM-alone (A3) provides modest correction (+3.5% over base); adding any calibrated\n  uncertainty signal (scalar, A4) gives a substantial additional jump (+2.6%); upgrading to per-DOF gives a further\n  incremental +2.1%. The combined uncertainty contribution (+4.7%) exceeds the residual-only contribution (+3.5%),\n  justifying the claim that calibrated uncertainty features are the primary driver — but the per-DOF vs scalar\n  margin is thin enough that \"per-DOF is additionally beneficial\" is more honest than \"per-DOF is essential.\"\n  COC is a positive but weak self-referential signal (ρ~0.38); reposition as \"promising\" rather than \"reliable,\"\n  with episode-level ACI as the pragmatic recommendation and COC as future work. SRPO stacking gain is real (~2.9%)\n  but near the kill threshold — frame as \"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\"\n  Per-perturbation breakdown is the honesty centerpiece: 4/7 dimensions show significant gain (camera, noise,\n  robot-state, lighting); layout and language see negligible improvement, honestly attributed to synthetic\n  augmentation's inability to cover these semantic dimensions. Still viable for CoRL poster with softened claims.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params).\n      CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state perturbation).\n      Evaluated on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks) and LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dimensions × 10 tasks).\n    expected_results:\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 92.6\n      libero_nominal_base: 94.8\n      libero_nominal_drop: 2.2\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 70.5\n      libero_plus_base: 62.3\n      libero_plus_gain_abs: 8.2\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 70.8\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 66.4\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 65.3\n      libero_plus_lighting: 71.2\n      libero_plus_background: 71.5\n      libero_plus_layout: 73.0\n      libero_plus_language: 72.4\n      crm_inference_overhead_ms: 3.2\n      cgs_k10_sampling_time_ms: 118\n      total_inference_time_per_step_ms: 134\n    result_rationale: >\n      Flow-matching samples from real VLA have covariance structure but noisier than synthetic MVP: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf\n      estimates are adequate but not tight, introducing ~15-20% feature quality degradation vs oracle covariance.\n      CRM still learns meaningful correction patterns because per-DOF std and conformal radii carry genuine\n      distributional information even with shrunk covariance. The 8.2% aggregate gain comes primarily from\n      camera/noise/robot-state dimensions where synthetic augmentation provides good calibration-set coverage —\n      CRM learns to suppress high-uncertainty DOFs (typically wrist rotation and z-translation under camera shift)\n      while preserving confident DOFs. The 2.2% nominal drop is CRM occasionally over-correcting in-distribution\n      actions where CGS correctly reports low uncertainty but CRM's learned residual still nudges slightly;\n      this is the cost of the correction module's expressivity. Gain is real but not as dramatic as the MVP's\n      ~27% per-DOF advantage because real VLA action distributions are higher-dimensional (7-DOF chunks vs 3D),\n      multimodal, and the covariance structure is harder to estimate reliably with K=10.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rates\"\n        content: |\n          | Method       | LIBERO (nominal) | LIBERO-Plus (agg.) | Δ Plus |\n          |-------------|------------------|-------------------|--------|\n          | SmolVLA     | 94.8             | 62.3              | —      |\n          | ACR-VLA     | 92.6             | 70.5              | +8.2   |\n      - name: \"Table 1 (extended): Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation  | SmolVLA | ACR-VLA | Δ     | Significant? |\n          |--------------|---------|---------|-------|-------------|\n          | camera_shift | 58.2    | 70.8    | +12.6 | ✓           |\n          | sensor_noise | 55.6    | 66.4    | +10.8 | ✓           |\n          | robot_state  | 56.8    | 65.3    | +8.5  | ✓           |\n          | lighting     | 65.4    | 71.2    | +5.8  | ✓           |\n          | background   | 68.3    | 71.5    | +3.2  | ✗           |\n          | layout       | 72.1    | 73.0    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n          | language     | 71.5    | 72.4    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Grouped bar chart — SmolVLA vs ACR-VLA success rate across 7 perturbation dimensions + nominal, sorted by ACR-VLA gain. Clear visual separation on camera/noise/robot-state, overlapping bars on layout/language.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Per-task heatmap (10 tasks × 7 perturbations) showing success rate delta. Reveals that gains are concentrated in manipulation-heavy tasks (e.g., pick-and-place, drawer) rather than navigation tasks.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 8.2% aggregate gain is solidly within the median range [6, 11]. The per-perturbation structure\n      is informative: the 4 dimensions with significant gain (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting) are\n      precisely those covered by synthetic augmentation, confirming that CGS calibration quality is the\n      bottleneck. Layout and language perturbations involve semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation\n      cannot cover — CGS features for these are essentially noise, and CRM correctly learns to make\n      near-zero corrections. The 2.2% nominal drop is acceptable for CoRL but should be discussed honestly.\n\n  - name: \"A3 Ablation — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation embeddings (from VLA encoder) as input, no CGS-derived uncertainty features.\n      Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual policy. Same CRM architecture (131K params),\n      same training data and procedure as full ACR-VLA.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 65.8\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 3.5\n      a3_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 4.7\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.8\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 63.1\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 60.2\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 60.9\n      libero_plus_lighting: 67.8\n      libero_plus_background: 69.5\n      libero_plus_layout: 72.6\n      libero_plus_language: 71.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without uncertainty features, CRM can only learn a fixed residual correction pattern conditioned on\n      visual observations. It captures some systematic biases (e.g., consistent z-offset under camera shift)\n      but cannot adapt correction magnitude or direction to per-step uncertainty. The 3.5% gain over base\n      reflects that observation-conditioned residuals capture low-hanging fruit — average correction vectors\n      that are right more often than wrong. But the lack of dimension-selective information means CRM applies\n      similar corrections to confident and uncertain DOFs alike, sometimes degrading already-accurate\n      dimensions. The 4.7% gap to full ACR-VLA (within [3,7]) confirms that conformal features provide\n      substantial value — almost as much as the residual module itself — but the gap isn't overwhelming,\n      consistent with the median tier's \"per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated\" characterization.\n      The kill criterion (gap < 2%) is clearly survived; Claim 1 holds with the softened framing.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A3)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs Full ACR-VLA |\n          |---------------------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | -8.2              |\n          | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | -4.7              |\n          | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | —                 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A3 bar sits between base and full ACR-VLA, visually demonstrating that conformal features account for roughly 57% of the total gain (4.7/8.2).\"\n    analysis: >\n      A3 gap of 4.7% clearly exceeds the 2% kill threshold. Claim 1 survives but the narrative must\n      acknowledge that observation-conditioned residuals alone provide non-trivial value (+3.5%). The\n      story should frame this as: \"CRM provides a correction mechanism; conformal features provide the\n      information that makes the correction dimension-selective and magnitude-appropriate.\"\n\n  - name: \"A4 Ablation — Scalar Uncertainty Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives observation embeddings + scalar uncertainty features (Euclidean distance from mean\n      to nearest calibration point, analogous to ReconVLA's approach) instead of 45D per-DOF features.\n      Scalar features: 1D distance + 1D log-likelihood + 1D normalized rank = 3D total.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 68.4\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 6.1\n      a4_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.1\n      a4_gap_to_a3: 2.6\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.2\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 67.5\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 63.8\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 63.0\n      libero_plus_lighting: 69.8\n      libero_plus_background: 70.4\n      libero_plus_layout: 72.8\n      libero_plus_language: 72.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty gives CRM a \"how uncertain overall\" signal, enabling it to modulate correction\n      magnitude globally — correct more aggressively when scalar uncertainty is high, less when low. This\n      captures the bulk of the uncertainty-conditioning benefit (+2.6% over A3), because the dominant\n      failure mode under distribution shift is \"everything is somewhat off\" rather than \"one specific DOF\n      is wrong.\" The remaining per-DOF advantage (+2.1%) comes from the minority of cases where uncertainty\n      is truly dimension-selective: e.g., camera shift primarily affecting x/y while z remains well-estimated,\n      or robot-state perturbation affecting specific joints. In these cases, per-DOF features allow CRM to\n      correct the problematic dimensions without disturbing the accurate ones. The 2.1% gap (within [1,3])\n      is modest but positive, supporting the softened claim that \"per-DOF provides incremental gains over scalar.\"\n      Most of the information-theoretic content of the 45D features is captured by the scalar summary — the\n      per-DOF structure adds refinement rather than fundamentally new capability.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A4)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs A3 | Δ vs Full |\n          |---------------------|-------------|-----------|---------|-----------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | —       | -8.2      |\n          | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | —       | -4.7      |\n          | A4: CRM + scalar   | 68.4        | +6.1      | +2.6    | -2.1      |\n          | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | +4.7    | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A4 bar closer to full ACR-VLA than to A3, visually showing that most of the 'uncertainty signal' value is captured by scalar, with per-DOF providing incremental lift.\"\n      - \"Fig 3 inset: Stacked contribution decomposition — CRM alone (3.5%), +scalar unc. (2.6%), +per-DOF upgrade (2.1%). Shows 'any uncertainty >> none' while per-DOF is beneficial but not dominant.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The A4 gap of 2.1% is within [1,3] as expected for median tier. The critical insight for the paper:\n      the decomposition base→A3→A4→Full shows gains of +3.5/+2.6/+2.1. The transition from \"no uncertainty\"\n      to \"any uncertainty\" (+2.6) is larger than \"scalar to per-DOF\" (+2.1), but per-DOF still provides\n      meaningful additional value. The narrative should emphasize that all three components contribute\n      and per-DOF is the final piece that enables truly dimension-selective correction.\n\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. New CGS calibration on SRPO samples (K=10).\n      SRPO already partially addresses robustness via training-time optimization — question is whether\n      inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_libero_plus: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_plus: 85.0\n      srpo_stacking_gain_abs: 2.9\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_nominal: 93.1\n      srpo_base_libero_nominal: 95.6\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_camera: 87.2\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_noise: 84.6\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_robot_state: 83.8\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_lighting: 85.4\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_background: 84.8\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_layout: 83.5\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_language: 83.2\n      crm_on_srpo_feature_quality_degradation_pct: 22\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO's training-time optimization partially collapses the flow-matching sample diversity — the model\n      is more deterministic post-finetuning, reducing the information content of K=10 samples. CGS features\n      extracted from SRPO samples have ~22% lower variance compared to base VLA samples, meaning the\n      uncertainty signal is attenuated. CRM still finds residual patterns to correct but the margin is\n      narrower because SRPO has already addressed many of the systematic biases that CRM would otherwise\n      correct. The 2.9% stacking gain (within [2,4]) comes from: (1) dimensions where SRPO's training\n      augmentation didn't cover the specific perturbation pattern (e.g., specific camera angle combinations),\n      and (2) high-uncertainty steps where SRPO's flow-matching still produces diffuse samples and CRM's\n      conformal features provide useful correction signal. The gain is near but above the 2% kill threshold,\n      supporting the claim \"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\" The 85.0%\n      stacked result (within [84,86]) would be a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: SRPO Stacking Results\"\n        content: |\n          | Method              | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus  |\n          |--------------------|---------------|-------------|---------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)     | 94.8          | 62.3        | —       |\n          | ACR-VLA            | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2    |\n          | SRPO               | 95.6          | 82.1        | +19.8   |\n          | SRPO + ACR-VLA     | 93.1          | 85.0        | +22.7   |\n          | Stacking gain      | -2.5          | +2.9        |         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Bar chart comparing SRPO vs SRPO+ACR-VLA across perturbation dimensions. Gains are small but consistent. Annotation highlighting 2.9% aggregate gain with 95% CI from 5 seeds.\"\n      - \"Fig 4 inset: CGS feature variance comparison — base VLA vs SRPO samples. Shows ~22% variance reduction, explaining attenuated per-DOF signal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.9% stacking gain survives the kill criterion (>2%) but barely. The paper should frame this\n      cautiously: \"ACR-VLA provides orthogonal value even atop training-time optimization, though the\n      margin is modest — consistent with SRPO having already resolved many of the uncertainty patterns\n      that ACR-VLA targets.\" The 85.0% SOTA claim is legitimate but should acknowledge the narrow margin.\n      Nominal drop of 2.5% on SRPO is slightly higher than on base VLA, possibly because SRPO's actions\n      are more precise and CRM's residuals are more likely to degrade already-good actions.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Three-part validation of Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI update signal:\n      (a) Spearman ρ between episode-level COC coverage fraction and binary task success,\n      (b) ACI alpha_t convergence trajectory over evaluation episodes,\n      (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison between ACI-adaptive and static calibration at matched marginal coverage.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.38\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.003\n      aci_convergence_episodes_to_0p02: 148\n      aci_target_coverage: 0.90\n      aci_achieved_coverage_at_convergence: 0.89\n      aci_overshoot_peak_alpha: 0.22\n      aci_steady_state_alpha: 0.11\n      ellipsoid_volume_ratio_adaptive_vs_static: 0.847\n      ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 15.3\n      static_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.90\n      adaptive_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.89\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC provides a per-step proxy for whether the model's predictions are self-consistent across\n      overlapping action chunks — high overlap agreement correlates with accurate predictions, low agreement\n      with uncertain/inaccurate ones. However, the correlation with task success is attenuated (ρ=0.38,\n      within [0.3, 0.5]) because: (1) task success is binary while COC is continuous, creating threshold\n      effects; (2) a single bad step can fail a task regardless of high average COC; (3) some tasks\n      succeed despite low COC through robustness of the underlying dynamics. The positive but weak\n      correlation means COC is a \"promising\" self-referential signal but not \"reliable\" enough to be\n      the primary ACI driver — episode-level success (when available) would be more informative.\n      ACI converges but slowly (~148 episodes, within expected range for weak signal) with initial\n      overshoot (alpha peaks at 0.22 before settling to 0.11) because COC-based updates have high\n      variance. The 15.3% ellipsoid narrowing (within [10, 20]) demonstrates that adaptive calibration\n      produces tighter prediction sets than static — same coverage guarantee but more informative\n      uncertainty regions. The narrowing is modest because K=10 covariance estimates limit how sharp\n      the ellipsoids can get.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: COC Signal Quality\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric                              | Value  |\n          |-------------------------------------|--------|\n          | Spearman ρ (COC ↔ success)          | 0.38   |\n          | p-value                              | 0.003  |\n          | ACI convergence (episodes to ±0.02) | 148    |\n          | ACI achieved coverage               | 0.89   |\n          | Ellipsoid narrowing vs static        | 15.3%  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5a: Scatter plot of episode-level COC coverage fraction (x) vs task success (y, jittered binary). Spearman ρ=0.38 annotated. Clear positive trend but substantial scatter.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: ACI convergence plot — alpha_t over 300 episodes with target coverage line (0.90). Shows initial overshoot, oscillation, and eventual convergence. Shaded band = 5 seed variance.\"\n      - \"Fig 5c: Ellipsoid volume CDF — adaptive (solid) vs static (dashed) at matched 90% coverage. Adaptive curve shifted left, demonstrating 15.3% tighter volumes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC is validated as a positive but weak signal. ρ=0.38 is statistically significant (p=0.003)\n      but far from the \"reliable\" threshold that would make COC a standalone monitoring tool. The paper\n      should position COC as: \"a step toward fully self-referential uncertainty monitoring that shows\n      promise but requires further development for deployment-grade reliability.\" ACI convergence\n      demonstrates the theoretical guarantee holds in practice, but the slow convergence and overshoot\n      suggest that COC's noisy signal makes online adaptation sluggish. The ellipsoid narrowing result\n      is the strongest evidence for ACI's value — same coverage with tighter sets means more informative\n      uncertainty features for CRM.\n\n  - name: \"A1 Ablation — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      Full CRM with per-DOF conformal features, but CGS thresholds fixed at calibration-set values —\n      no online ACI adaptation. COC is not computed. Tests the marginal contribution of ACI.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 67.8\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 5.5\n      a1_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.7\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.4\n      static_coverage_at_early_episodes: 0.93\n      static_coverage_at_late_episodes: 0.82\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without ACI, conformal thresholds are calibrated on the training distribution and remain fixed.\n      Under LIBERO-Plus perturbations, the calibration set is no longer representative: early evaluation\n      episodes see conservative over-coverage (0.93 vs target 0.90) because perturbations haven't fully\n      diverged from calibration conditions, but later episodes see under-coverage (0.82) as distribution\n      shift accumulates. This miscalibration means CRM receives systematically biased uncertainty features —\n      over-estimated for some perturbation types, under-estimated for others. The 2.7% gap to full ACR-VLA\n      reflects that ACI's online correction produces better-calibrated inputs for CRM, particularly for\n      perturbation dimensions where the shift is gradual and ACI can track it (camera, lighting). For\n      abrupt shifts (layout, language), ACI has less time to adapt, so the gap is smaller there.\n      The result validates ACI's contribution but shows it's not the dominant factor — static conformal\n      features still carry most of the correction signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A1)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Full |\n          |-----------------------|-------------|-----------|\n          | A1: CRM, static CGS  | 67.8        | -2.7      |\n          | Full ACR-VLA          | 70.5        | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation chart). Additionally: supplementary figure showing coverage drift over episodes — static (drifting down) vs ACI-adaptive (hovering near 0.90).\"\n    analysis: >\n      ACI contributes ~2.7% absolute, which is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features\n      themselves. The coverage drift analysis (0.93→0.82 for static) provides a clean mechanistic\n      explanation for why adaptive calibration helps. This supports Claim 2 but with caveats —\n      ACI is beneficial but not transformative.\n\n  - name: \"A2 Ablation — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + ACI pipeline but instead of CRM, uses multivariate-CP-guided sample selection\n      (extend ReconVLA's approach to leverage per-DOF conformal radii for selecting best sample\n      from K=10). No learned correction, just informed selection.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 66.9\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 4.6\n      a2_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 3.6\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 94.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection with multivariate CP features is a strict improvement over ReconVLA's scalar\n      selection — per-DOF conformal radii enable selecting the sample that is best specifically in\n      the most-uncertain dimensions, rather than best in aggregate Euclidean distance. The 4.6% gain\n      over base is substantial and validates that CGS features contain useful information. However,\n      selection is fundamentally limited to choosing among the K=10 samples — it cannot synthesize\n      an action better than any individual sample, which is CRM's advantage. The 3.6% gap to full\n      ACR-VLA quantifies CRM's contribution as a learned synthesizer. Notably, the 0.6% nominal drop\n      is much smaller than full ACR-VLA's 2.2% — because sample selection is conservative (it only\n      picks from generated samples, never modifying them), it's less likely to degrade in-distribution\n      performance. This creates an interesting trade-off for the paper to discuss.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A2)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant               | LIBERO-Plus | LIBERO (nom.) | Δ Plus vs Full |\n          |----------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------|\n          | A2: Selection only   | 66.9        | 94.2          | -3.6           |\n          | Full ACR-VLA         | 70.5        | 92.6          | —              |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3. A2 bar annotated with note: 'selection-only — lower OOD gain but minimal nominal drop.'\"\n    analysis: >\n      A2 provides a useful reference point — it validates that CGS features enable better sample\n      selection (extending ReconVLA), and quantifies CRM's value as a learned corrector (+3.6%).\n      The nominal preservation advantage of A2 is worth discussing as a practical deployment\n      consideration: if nominal drop must be minimized, selection-only is safer.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation-Dimension Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Detailed ACR-VLA gain analysis across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions, evaluated\n      per-task (10 tasks × 7 dimensions = 70 cells). Statistical significance via paired\n      bootstrap (1000 resamples, α=0.05) over 5 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      camera_shift_gain: 12.6\n      camera_shift_p_value: 0.001\n      sensor_noise_gain: 10.8\n      sensor_noise_p_value: 0.002\n      robot_state_gain: 8.5\n      robot_state_p_value: 0.008\n      lighting_gain: 5.8\n      lighting_p_value: 0.031\n      background_gain: 3.2\n      background_p_value: 0.112\n      layout_gain: 0.9\n      layout_p_value: 0.483\n      language_gain: 0.9\n      language_p_value: 0.501\n      dims_with_significant_gain: 4\n      dims_significant_list: \"camera_shift, sensor_noise, robot_state, lighting\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The per-perturbation structure directly reflects synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter,\n      Gaussian sensor noise, and robot-state perturbation are all covered by synthetic augmentation —\n      CGS sees similar distribution shifts during calibration, producing well-calibrated conformal features\n      that CRM can exploit. Camera shift shows the largest gain (+12.6%) because it primarily affects\n      translational DOFs (x, y) where per-DOF conformal features are most discriminative — camera shift\n      makes position uncertain while orientation remains relatively stable, and per-DOF features capture\n      this asymmetry. Lighting (+5.8%) reaches significance because brightness changes create predictable\n      patterns in flow-matching sample variance. Background changes (+3.2%) cause modest, diffuse\n      uncertainty increase that CRM partially addresses, but the effect is not statistically significant\n      at α=0.05. Layout (+0.9%) and language (+0.9%) are essentially unaddressed because these are\n      semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — the calibration set contains no examples\n      of rearranged furniture or paraphrased instructions, so CGS features under these perturbations\n      are poorly calibrated and CRM cannot extract useful correction signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gains\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation   | Base  | ACR-VLA | Δ     | p-value | Sig.? |\n          |---------------|-------|---------|-------|---------|-------|\n          | camera_shift  | 58.2  | 70.8    | +12.6 | 0.001   | ✓     |\n          | sensor_noise  | 55.6  | 66.4    | +10.8 | 0.002   | ✓     |\n          | robot_state   | 56.8  | 65.3    | +8.5  | 0.008   | ✓     |\n          | lighting      | 65.4  | 71.2    | +5.8  | 0.031   | ✓     |\n          | background    | 68.3  | 71.5    | +3.2  | 0.112   | ✗     |\n          | layout        | 72.1  | 73.0    | +0.9  | 0.483   | ✗     |\n          | language      | 71.5  | 72.4    | +0.9  | 0.501   | ✗     |\n      - name: \"Table 4b: Per-Task × Per-Perturbation Heatmap Data (top-5 tasks by gain)\"\n        content: |\n          | Task              | camera | noise | robot | light | bg   | layout | lang |\n          |-------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|------|--------|------|\n          | pick_place_can    | +18.0  | +14.0 | +12.0 | +8.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\n          | open_drawer       | +16.0  | +12.0 | +10.0 | +6.0  | +4.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\n          | stack_blocks      | +14.0  | +14.0 | +10.0 | +8.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +0.0 |\n          | close_microwave   | +12.0  | +10.0 | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\n          | turn_faucet       | +10.0  | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Radar chart showing ACR-VLA gain (%) across 7 perturbation dimensions. Clear asymmetry — gain concentrated in camera/noise/robot-state lobe, flat on layout/language.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: 10×7 heatmap of per-task per-perturbation success rate deltas. Reveals task-perturbation interaction patterns.\"\n      - \"Fig 6c: Correlation between synthetic augmentation coverage (measured as calibration set overlap with each perturbation) and ACR-VLA gain. Strong positive correlation (r=0.91).\"\n    analysis: >\n      4/7 dimensions with significant gain is within [3, 5]. This result is central to the median-tier\n      narrative: ACR-VLA works well where calibration coverage exists, honestly fails where it doesn't.\n      The paper should present this transparency as a strength — \"we clearly identify the boundary of\n      our method's applicability and provide a mechanistic explanation.\" The correlation between\n      augmentation coverage and gain (r=0.91) is a strong explanatory result that reviewers will\n      appreciate. Layout/language as honest limitations, with ACI online adaptation as partial mitigation\n      and future work on semantic augmentation as natural extension.\n\n  - name: \"A5 Ablation — CGS Joint Mahalanobis vs Per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare full CGS (joint Mahalanobis with Ledoit-Wolf shrunk covariance) vs per-DOF independent\n      Bonferroni-corrected intervals. Tests whether multivariate CP's joint structure provides value\n      beyond simply having per-DOF information.\n    expected_results:\n      joint_mahalanobis_libero_plus: 70.5\n      bonferroni_libero_plus: 69.1\n      gap_joint_vs_bonferroni: 1.4\n      bonferroni_coverage: 0.94\n      joint_coverage: 0.90\n      bonferroni_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 1.0  # normalized\n      joint_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 0.72\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bonferroni correction treats each DOF independently and inflates thresholds to maintain joint\n      coverage — producing conservative, hypercubic confidence regions that ignore cross-DOF covariance.\n      Joint Mahalanobis with shrunk covariance produces ellipsoidal regions aligned with the actual\n      covariance structure, yielding tighter (28% smaller volume) regions at the same coverage level.\n      The 1.4% performance gap comes from CRM receiving more informative features: joint CGS features\n      encode which DOF pairs co-vary (e.g., x and y under camera shift), enabling CRM to make correlated\n      corrections across related DOFs. With Bonferroni, CRM sees each DOF's uncertainty independently\n      and must re-learn any cross-DOF correction patterns from the observation embedding alone. The gap\n      is modest because K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is noisy — the theoretical advantage of joint\n      Mahalanobis is attenuated by covariance estimation error, and Bonferroni's conservative margins\n      partially compensate by providing wider \"safety padding.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A5 Ablation Results\"\n        content: |\n          | CGS Variant         | LIBERO-Plus | Coverage | Avg. Volume |\n          |--------------------|-------------|----------|-------------|\n          | Joint Mahalanobis  | 70.5        | 0.90     | 0.72 (norm) |\n          | Per-DOF Bonferroni | 69.1        | 0.94     | 1.00 (norm) |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S1: 2D slices of confidence regions — ellipsoidal (joint) vs rectangular (Bonferroni) for example action dimensions. Shows tighter ellipsoidal regions capturing covariance tilt.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.4% gap is small but validates that joint multivariate CP provides value through\n      covariance-aware regions. Worth reporting as a supplementary ablation to support the\n      multivariate CP design choice. The over-coverage of Bonferroni (0.94 vs target 0.90) is\n      an inherent inefficiency that the joint approach avoids.\n\n  - name: \"A6 Ablation — COC Signal vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare ACI driven by COC (self-referential, per-step) vs ACI driven by episode-level binary\n      task success (stronger signal, but requires ground-truth outcome and only updates once per episode).\n    expected_results:\n      coc_based_aci_libero_plus: 70.5\n      episode_based_aci_libero_plus: 71.8\n      gap_episode_vs_coc: 1.3\n      coc_aci_convergence_episodes: 148\n      episode_aci_convergence_episodes: 42\n      coc_aci_coverage_variance: 0.018\n      episode_aci_coverage_variance: 0.006\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level success is a stronger, lower-variance signal for ACI — it directly reflects whether\n      the uncertainty calibration led to good outcomes. COC is noisy because chunk overlap consistency\n      is only a proxy for prediction quality, and individual step-level COC values have high variance.\n      The 1.3% gap reflects that episode-based ACI converges ~3.5x faster (42 vs 148 episodes) and\n      maintains tighter coverage tracking (variance 0.006 vs 0.018). However, episode-level success\n      requires ground-truth task outcomes — available in simulation but not in real deployment. This\n      is the key argument for COC: it's self-referential (no external supervision). The paper should\n      present this as a trade-off: \"episode-level ACI is preferable when outcomes are available;\n      COC-based ACI sacrifices ~1.3% performance for full self-referentiality.\" In the median tier,\n      COC's disadvantage is noticeable but not disqualifying — it still converges and provides meaningful\n      adaptation, just more slowly and with more variance.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A6 Ablation: ACI Signal Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | ACI Signal     | LIBERO-Plus | Convergence (eps) | Coverage Var. |\n          |---------------|-------------|-------------------|---------------|\n          | COC (self-ref)| 70.5        | 148               | 0.018         |\n          | Episode success| 71.8       | 42                | 0.006         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S2: Side-by-side ACI convergence plots — COC-driven (noisy, slow) vs episode-driven (smooth, fast). Both converge to target coverage but with very different dynamics.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Episode-based ACI is clearly superior when ground-truth is available. COC's value proposition\n      is self-referentiality for deployment scenarios without outcome feedback. The 1.3% gap and 3.5x\n      convergence slowdown are honest costs of self-referentiality. Paper recommendation: use episode-level\n      ACI as the default in simulation experiments; present COC as a deployment-oriented variant with\n      known performance trade-offs. This supports repositioning COC as \"promising future work\" rather\n      than the primary ACI signal.\n\n  - name: \"A7 Ablation — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replace CGS epistemic uncertainty features (45D) with DiU-style aleatoric uncertainty features\n      (7D per-DOF variance from single forward pass). Also test combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D).\n      Validates that epistemic (distribution-shift-sensitive) features are more useful than aleatoric\n      (in-distribution-also-high) features for OOD correction.\n    expected_results:\n      cgs_epistemic_libero_plus: 70.5\n      diu_aleatoric_libero_plus: 67.4\n      combined_52d_libero_plus: 71.1\n      gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric: 3.1\n      cgs_epistemic_libero_nominal: 92.6\n      diu_aleatoric_libero_nominal: 93.9\n      combined_52d_libero_nominal: 92.4\n      diu_aleatoric_in_dist_variance: 0.048\n      cgs_epistemic_in_dist_variance: 0.012\n      diu_aleatoric_ood_variance: 0.063\n      cgs_epistemic_ood_variance: 0.089\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU captures aleatoric uncertainty — inherent action ambiguity that exists even in-distribution.\n      Under distribution shift, DiU's signal increases only modestly (+31%, 0.048→0.063) because most of\n      what DiU measures is irreducible noise. CGS epistemic features, in contrast, are low in-distribution\n      (0.012) and spike dramatically under shift (+642%, 0.012→0.089), providing a clear signal for \"this\n      is out-of-distribution.\" CRM conditioned on epistemic features can discriminate OOD steps that need\n      correction from in-distribution steps that don't. With aleatoric features, CRM sees consistently\n      elevated uncertainty everywhere, reducing its ability to be selective. The 3.1% gap validates the\n      epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction. The combined 52D features provide a marginal +0.6% over epistemic\n      alone — aleatoric features add some complementary information about inherent action ambiguity (useful\n      for multi-modal tasks like placing objects with rotational symmetry), but the gain is small because\n      most of the relevant signal is already captured by epistemic features. The lower nominal drop of DiU\n      (0.9% vs 2.2% for CGS) reflects that aleatoric features are less \"aggressive\" — they don't provoke\n      large corrections because their signal doesn't differentiate ID from OOD.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A7 Ablation: Feature Type Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Type        | Dim | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus vs Base |\n          |--------------------|-----|---------------|-------------|----------------|\n          | CGS epistemic       | 45  | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2           |\n          | DiU aleatoric       | 7   | 93.9          | 67.4        | +5.1           |\n          | Combined            | 52  | 92.4          | 71.1        | +8.8           |\n      - name: \"A7: Signal Discrimination\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Source | In-Dist Var. | OOD Var. | Ratio (OOD/ID) |\n          |--------------|-------------|---------|----------------|\n          | CGS epistemic | 0.012       | 0.089   | 7.42           |\n          | DiU aleatoric | 0.048       | 0.063   | 1.31           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S3a: Violin plots of feature magnitude distributions (ID vs OOD) for epistemic and aleatoric features. CGS shows clear bimodal separation; DiU shows overlapping distributions.\"\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S3b: CRM correction magnitude conditioned on feature type — epistemic-conditioned CRM applies larger corrections OOD and smaller ID; aleatoric-conditioned CRM applies similar corrections everywhere.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 7.42x OOD/ID ratio for epistemic features vs 1.31x for aleatoric validates the core design\n      choice. This is one of the cleaner results — it mechanistically explains why flow-matching sample\n      diversity (epistemic) is more informative than single-pass variance (aleatoric) for distribution\n      shift detection. The combined 52D result (+0.6% over epistemic alone) suggests that adding aleatoric\n      features is marginally beneficial — worth mentioning but not worth the complexity for most applications.\n\n  - name: \"Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient-based feature importance: compute |∂correction/∂feature| averaged over evaluation episodes,\n      broken down by perturbation type. Identifies which of the 45 CGS features drive CRM's corrections\n      for each perturbation dimension.\n    expected_results:\n      top_features_camera_shift: \"per-DOF std (x, y) — 34% of total gradient; conformal radius (x, y) — 22%\"\n      top_features_sensor_noise: \"per-DOF std (all DOFs) — 41%; ellipsoid volume — 18%\"\n      top_features_robot_state: \"conformal radius (joint 1-4) — 38%; per-DOF std (joint 1-4) — 25%\"\n      top_features_lighting: \"per-DOF std (x, y, z) — 29%; ellipsoid volume — 21%\"\n      per_dof_std_importance_share: 0.42\n      conformal_radius_importance_share: 0.31\n      ellipsoid_volume_importance_share: 0.14\n      cross_dof_covariance_importance_share: 0.13\n    result_rationale: >\n      Per-DOF std features are the most important (42% of gradient magnitude) because they directly encode\n      \"how uncertain is this specific DOF\" — the most actionable information for dimension-selective\n      correction. Conformal radius features rank second (31%) because they encode calibrated confidence\n      intervals that are more robust than raw std to distributional assumptions. Ellipsoid volume (14%) is\n      a global uncertainty summary — useful for CRM's gating mechanism (how much total correction to apply)\n      but less informative for dimension selection. Cross-DOF covariance features (13%) capture correlation\n      structure; their moderate importance reflects that K=10 covariance estimates are noisy, limiting\n      the information content of off-diagonal terms. The perturbation-specific patterns are illuminating:\n      camera shift activates translational DOF features (x, y) because it primarily affects workspace\n      position estimation; robot-state perturbation activates joint-specific conformal radii because it\n      directly perturbs joint configurations; sensor noise activates broad std features because it\n      diffusely increases all DOF uncertainties.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Feature Importance by Category\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Category    | Importance Share | Primary Perturbations |\n          |--------------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n          | Per-DOF std (7D)   | 42%             | noise, camera, light |\n          | Conformal radius (7D)| 31%           | robot-state, camera  |\n          | Ellipsoid volume (1D)| 14%           | all (gating)         |\n          | Cross-DOF cov (30D) | 13%            | robot-state          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Stacked bar chart — feature importance share by category, one bar per perturbation dimension. Shows distinct feature activation patterns per perturbation type.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: 45-feature × 7-perturbation heatmap of gradient magnitudes. Reveals sparse, interpretable activation patterns — CRM learns perturbation-specific feature usage without explicit supervision.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The feature importance analysis validates the dimension-selective correction mechanism: CRM\n      learns to attend to different uncertainty features for different perturbation types, without\n      explicit supervision about which perturbation is active. This is a compelling interpretability\n      result for the paper. The 42%+31% = 73% importance of per-DOF features (std + conformal radius)\n      supports Claim 1's emphasis on per-DOF structure, even though the A4 ablation showed only +2.1%\n      over scalar — the mechanism is clearly there, even if the aggregate performance gain is modest.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace K=10 full denoising trajectories with K=10 single-step distilled generation via\n      SnapFlow-style consistency distillation. Test: (a) inference speedup, (b) sample diversity\n      preservation, (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance.\n    expected_results:\n      full_denoising_inference_ms: 118\n      snapflow_1step_inference_ms: 38\n      speedup_factor: 3.1\n      diversity_trace_ratio: 0.83\n      full_denoising_libero_plus: 70.5\n      snapflow_libero_plus: 69.8\n      performance_drop: 0.7\n      snapflow_covariance_frobenius_error: 0.21\n    result_rationale: >\n      Single-step distilled generation produces samples ~3.1x faster but with reduced diversity:\n      the trace ratio of 0.83 means 1-step samples capture 83% of the covariance structure of\n      full denoising samples. The 17% diversity loss comes from the distillation process collapsing\n      minor modes in the action distribution — the dominant mode is well-preserved but tail samples\n      are pulled inward. This propagates to a modest 0.7% ACR-VLA performance drop because the\n      CGS features computed from less-diverse samples have slightly attenuated covariance structure,\n      reducing the per-DOF signal quality. The Frobenius error of 0.21 (normalized) in covariance\n      estimation means the Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage target absorbs more weight, further compressing the\n      estimated covariance toward the identity-like shrinkage target. The practical trade-off is\n      favorable: 3.1x speedup for 0.7% accuracy cost, making real-time deployment more feasible.\n      This is a supplementary result that strengthens the practical narrative without affecting claims.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        content: |\n          | Sampling Method     | K=10 Time (ms) | Diversity | LIBERO-Plus | Δ   |\n          |--------------------|----------------|-----------|-------------|-----|\n          | Full denoising     | 118            | 1.00      | 70.5        | —   |\n          | SnapFlow 1-step    | 38             | 0.83      | 69.8        | -0.7|\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S4: Pareto plot — inference time vs LIBERO-Plus success rate. SnapFlow point near the Pareto frontier, showing favorable speed-accuracy trade-off.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration is a practical engineering contribution. The 0.7% drop is small enough\n      that the 3.1x speedup is clearly worthwhile for deployment. The diversity reduction is the\n      primary concern — if future work increases K beyond 10, SnapFlow's diversity loss may compound.\n      Report as supplementary result demonstrating deployment feasibility.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11182,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11182},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef3a4a7f-e7d7-4354-91e3-42aa53e20677","uuid":"1cc63480-4b5a-40cc-8ea9-bcce0426e1b2"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":363890,"duration_api_ms":365114,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated relative to synthetic MVP. Story pivots from \"per-DOF is the key innovation\"\n  to \"structured uncertainty conditioning (including per-DOF) significantly improves inference-time correction.\"\n  Ablation decomposition: CRM-alone (A3) provides modest correction (+3.5% over base); adding any calibrated\n  uncertainty signal (scalar, A4) gives a substantial additional jump (+2.6%); upgrading to per-DOF gives a further\n  incremental +2.1%. The combined uncertainty contribution (+4.7%) exceeds the residual-only contribution (+3.5%),\n  justifying the claim that calibrated uncertainty features are the primary driver — but the per-DOF vs scalar\n  margin is thin enough that \"per-DOF is additionally beneficial\" is more honest than \"per-DOF is essential.\"\n  COC is a positive but weak self-referential signal (ρ~0.38); reposition as \"promising\" rather than \"reliable,\"\n  with episode-level ACI as the pragmatic recommendation and COC as future work. SRPO stacking gain is real (~2.9%)\n  but near the kill threshold — frame as \"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\"\n  Per-perturbation breakdown is the honesty centerpiece: 4/7 dimensions show significant gain (camera, noise,\n  robot-state, lighting); layout and language see negligible improvement, honestly attributed to synthetic\n  augmentation's inability to cover these semantic dimensions. Still viable for CoRL poster with softened claims.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params).\n      CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state perturbation).\n      Evaluated on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks) and LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dimensions × 10 tasks).\n    expected_results:\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 92.6\n      libero_nominal_base: 94.8\n      libero_nominal_drop: 2.2\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 70.5\n      libero_plus_base: 62.3\n      libero_plus_gain_abs: 8.2\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 70.8\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 66.4\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 65.3\n      libero_plus_lighting: 71.2\n      libero_plus_background: 71.5\n      libero_plus_layout: 73.0\n      libero_plus_language: 72.4\n      crm_inference_overhead_ms: 3.2\n      cgs_k10_sampling_time_ms: 118\n      total_inference_time_per_step_ms: 134\n    result_rationale: >\n      Flow-matching samples from real VLA have covariance structure but noisier than synthetic MVP: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf\n      estimates are adequate but not tight, introducing ~15-20% feature quality degradation vs oracle covariance.\n      CRM still learns meaningful correction patterns because per-DOF std and conformal radii carry genuine\n      distributional information even with shrunk covariance. The 8.2% aggregate gain comes primarily from\n      camera/noise/robot-state dimensions where synthetic augmentation provides good calibration-set coverage —\n      CRM learns to suppress high-uncertainty DOFs (typically wrist rotation and z-translation under camera shift)\n      while preserving confident DOFs. The 2.2% nominal drop is CRM occasionally over-correcting in-distribution\n      actions where CGS correctly reports low uncertainty but CRM's learned residual still nudges slightly;\n      this is the cost of the correction module's expressivity. Gain is real but not as dramatic as the MVP's\n      ~27% per-DOF advantage because real VLA action distributions are higher-dimensional (7-DOF chunks vs 3D),\n      multimodal, and the covariance structure is harder to estimate reliably with K=10.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rates\"\n        content: |\n          | Method       | LIBERO (nominal) | LIBERO-Plus (agg.) | Δ Plus |\n          |-------------|------------------|-------------------|--------|\n          | SmolVLA     | 94.8             | 62.3              | —      |\n          | ACR-VLA     | 92.6             | 70.5              | +8.2   |\n      - name: \"Table 1 (extended): Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation  | SmolVLA | ACR-VLA | Δ     | Significant? |\n          |--------------|---------|---------|-------|-------------|\n          | camera_shift | 58.2    | 70.8    | +12.6 | ✓           |\n          | sensor_noise | 55.6    | 66.4    | +10.8 | ✓           |\n          | robot_state  | 56.8    | 65.3    | +8.5  | ✓           |\n          | lighting     | 65.4    | 71.2    | +5.8  | ✓           |\n          | background   | 68.3    | 71.5    | +3.2  | ✗           |\n          | layout       | 72.1    | 73.0    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n          | language     | 71.5    | 72.4    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Grouped bar chart — SmolVLA vs ACR-VLA success rate across 7 perturbation dimensions + nominal, sorted by ACR-VLA gain. Clear visual separation on camera/noise/robot-state, overlapping bars on layout/language.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Per-task heatmap (10 tasks × 7 perturbations) showing success rate delta. Reveals that gains are concentrated in manipulation-heavy tasks (e.g., pick-and-place, drawer) rather than navigation tasks.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 8.2% aggregate gain is solidly within the median range [6, 11]. The per-perturbation structure\n      is informative: the 4 dimensions with significant gain (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting) are\n      precisely those covered by synthetic augmentation, confirming that CGS calibration quality is the\n      bottleneck. Layout and language perturbations involve semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation\n      cannot cover — CGS features for these are essentially noise, and CRM correctly learns to make\n      near-zero corrections. The 2.2% nominal drop is acceptable for CoRL but should be discussed honestly.\n\n  - name: \"A3 Ablation — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation embeddings (from VLA encoder) as input, no CGS-derived uncertainty features.\n      Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual policy. Same CRM architecture (131K params),\n      same training data and procedure as full ACR-VLA.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 65.8\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 3.5\n      a3_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 4.7\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.8\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 63.1\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 60.2\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 60.9\n      libero_plus_lighting: 67.8\n      libero_plus_background: 69.5\n      libero_plus_layout: 72.6\n      libero_plus_language: 71.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without uncertainty features, CRM can only learn a fixed residual correction pattern conditioned on\n      visual observations. It captures some systematic biases (e.g., consistent z-offset under camera shift)\n      but cannot adapt correction magnitude or direction to per-step uncertainty. The 3.5% gain over base\n      reflects that observation-conditioned residuals capture low-hanging fruit — average correction vectors\n      that are right more often than wrong. But the lack of dimension-selective information means CRM applies\n      similar corrections to confident and uncertain DOFs alike, sometimes degrading already-accurate\n      dimensions. The 4.7% gap to full ACR-VLA (within [3,7]) confirms that conformal features provide\n      substantial value — almost as much as the residual module itself — but the gap isn't overwhelming,\n      consistent with the median tier's \"per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated\" characterization.\n      The kill criterion (gap < 2%) is clearly survived; Claim 1 holds with the softened framing.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A3)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs Full ACR-VLA |\n          |---------------------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | -8.2              |\n          | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | -4.7              |\n          | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | —                 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A3 bar sits between base and full ACR-VLA, visually demonstrating that conformal features account for roughly 57% of the total gain (4.7/8.2).\"\n    analysis: >\n      A3 gap of 4.7% clearly exceeds the 2% kill threshold. Claim 1 survives but the narrative must\n      acknowledge that observation-conditioned residuals alone provide non-trivial value (+3.5%). The\n      story should frame this as: \"CRM provides a correction mechanism; conformal features provide the\n      information that makes the correction dimension-selective and magnitude-appropriate.\"\n\n  - name: \"A4 Ablation — Scalar Uncertainty Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives observation embeddings + scalar uncertainty features (Euclidean distance from mean\n      to nearest calibration point, analogous to ReconVLA's approach) instead of 45D per-DOF features.\n      Scalar features: 1D distance + 1D log-likelihood + 1D normalized rank = 3D total.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 68.4\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 6.1\n      a4_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.1\n      a4_gap_to_a3: 2.6\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.2\n      libero_plus_camera_shift: 67.5\n      libero_plus_sensor_noise: 63.8\n      libero_plus_robot_state: 63.0\n      libero_plus_lighting: 69.8\n      libero_plus_background: 70.4\n      libero_plus_layout: 72.8\n      libero_plus_language: 72.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty gives CRM a \"how uncertain overall\" signal, enabling it to modulate correction\n      magnitude globally — correct more aggressively when scalar uncertainty is high, less when low. This\n      captures the bulk of the uncertainty-conditioning benefit (+2.6% over A3), because the dominant\n      failure mode under distribution shift is \"everything is somewhat off\" rather than \"one specific DOF\n      is wrong.\" The remaining per-DOF advantage (+2.1%) comes from the minority of cases where uncertainty\n      is truly dimension-selective: e.g., camera shift primarily affecting x/y while z remains well-estimated,\n      or robot-state perturbation affecting specific joints. In these cases, per-DOF features allow CRM to\n      correct the problematic dimensions without disturbing the accurate ones. The 2.1% gap (within [1,3])\n      is modest but positive, supporting the softened claim that \"per-DOF provides incremental gains over scalar.\"\n      Most of the information-theoretic content of the 45D features is captured by the scalar summary — the\n      per-DOF structure adds refinement rather than fundamentally new capability.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A4)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs A3 | Δ vs Full |\n          |---------------------|-------------|-----------|---------|-----------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | —       | -8.2      |\n          | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | —       | -4.7      |\n          | A4: CRM + scalar   | 68.4        | +6.1      | +2.6    | -2.1      |\n          | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | +4.7    | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A4 bar closer to full ACR-VLA than to A3, visually showing that most of the 'uncertainty signal' value is captured by scalar, with per-DOF providing incremental lift.\"\n      - \"Fig 3 inset: Stacked contribution decomposition — CRM alone (3.5%), +scalar unc. (2.6%), +per-DOF upgrade (2.1%). Shows 'any uncertainty >> none' while per-DOF is beneficial but not dominant.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The A4 gap of 2.1% is within [1,3] as expected for median tier. The critical insight for the paper:\n      the decomposition base→A3→A4→Full shows gains of +3.5/+2.6/+2.1. The transition from \"no uncertainty\"\n      to \"any uncertainty\" (+2.6) is larger than \"scalar to per-DOF\" (+2.1), but per-DOF still provides\n      meaningful additional value. The narrative should emphasize that all three components contribute\n      and per-DOF is the final piece that enables truly dimension-selective correction.\n\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. New CGS calibration on SRPO samples (K=10).\n      SRPO already partially addresses robustness via training-time optimization — question is whether\n      inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_libero_plus: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_plus: 85.0\n      srpo_stacking_gain_abs: 2.9\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_nominal: 93.1\n      srpo_base_libero_nominal: 95.6\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_camera: 87.2\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_noise: 84.6\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_robot_state: 83.8\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_lighting: 85.4\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_background: 84.8\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_layout: 83.5\n      srpo_plus_acr_vla_language: 83.2\n      crm_on_srpo_feature_quality_degradation_pct: 22\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO's training-time optimization partially collapses the flow-matching sample diversity — the model\n      is more deterministic post-finetuning, reducing the information content of K=10 samples. CGS features\n      extracted from SRPO samples have ~22% lower variance compared to base VLA samples, meaning the\n      uncertainty signal is attenuated. CRM still finds residual patterns to correct but the margin is\n      narrower because SRPO has already addressed many of the systematic biases that CRM would otherwise\n      correct. The 2.9% stacking gain (within [2,4]) comes from: (1) dimensions where SRPO's training\n      augmentation didn't cover the specific perturbation pattern (e.g., specific camera angle combinations),\n      and (2) high-uncertainty steps where SRPO's flow-matching still produces diffuse samples and CRM's\n      conformal features provide useful correction signal. The gain is near but above the 2% kill threshold,\n      supporting the claim \"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\" The 85.0%\n      stacked result (within [84,86]) would be a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: SRPO Stacking Results\"\n        content: |\n          | Method              | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus  |\n          |--------------------|---------------|-------------|---------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)     | 94.8          | 62.3        | —       |\n          | ACR-VLA            | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2    |\n          | SRPO               | 95.6          | 82.1        | +19.8   |\n          | SRPO + ACR-VLA     | 93.1          | 85.0        | +22.7   |\n          | Stacking gain      | -2.5          | +2.9        |         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Bar chart comparing SRPO vs SRPO+ACR-VLA across perturbation dimensions. Gains are small but consistent. Annotation highlighting 2.9% aggregate gain with 95% CI from 5 seeds.\"\n      - \"Fig 4 inset: CGS feature variance comparison — base VLA vs SRPO samples. Shows ~22% variance reduction, explaining attenuated per-DOF signal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.9% stacking gain survives the kill criterion (>2%) but barely. The paper should frame this\n      cautiously: \"ACR-VLA provides orthogonal value even atop training-time optimization, though the\n      margin is modest — consistent with SRPO having already resolved many of the uncertainty patterns\n      that ACR-VLA targets.\" The 85.0% SOTA claim is legitimate but should acknowledge the narrow margin.\n      Nominal drop of 2.5% on SRPO is slightly higher than on base VLA, possibly because SRPO's actions\n      are more precise and CRM's residuals are more likely to degrade already-good actions.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Three-part validation of Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI update signal:\n      (a) Spearman ρ between episode-level COC coverage fraction and binary task success,\n      (b) ACI alpha_t convergence trajectory over evaluation episodes,\n      (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison between ACI-adaptive and static calibration at matched marginal coverage.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.38\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.003\n      aci_convergence_episodes_to_0p02: 148\n      aci_target_coverage: 0.90\n      aci_achieved_coverage_at_convergence: 0.89\n      aci_overshoot_peak_alpha: 0.22\n      aci_steady_state_alpha: 0.11\n      ellipsoid_volume_ratio_adaptive_vs_static: 0.847\n      ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 15.3\n      static_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.90\n      adaptive_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.89\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC provides a per-step proxy for whether the model's predictions are self-consistent across\n      overlapping action chunks — high overlap agreement correlates with accurate predictions, low agreement\n      with uncertain/inaccurate ones. However, the correlation with task success is attenuated (ρ=0.38,\n      within [0.3, 0.5]) because: (1) task success is binary while COC is continuous, creating threshold\n      effects; (2) a single bad step can fail a task regardless of high average COC; (3) some tasks\n      succeed despite low COC through robustness of the underlying dynamics. The positive but weak\n      correlation means COC is a \"promising\" self-referential signal but not \"reliable\" enough to be\n      the primary ACI driver — episode-level success (when available) would be more informative.\n      ACI converges but slowly (~148 episodes, within expected range for weak signal) with initial\n      overshoot (alpha peaks at 0.22 before settling to 0.11) because COC-based updates have high\n      variance. The 15.3% ellipsoid narrowing (within [10, 20]) demonstrates that adaptive calibration\n      produces tighter prediction sets than static — same coverage guarantee but more informative\n      uncertainty regions. The narrowing is modest because K=10 covariance estimates limit how sharp\n      the ellipsoids can get.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: COC Signal Quality\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric                              | Value  |\n          |-------------------------------------|--------|\n          | Spearman ρ (COC ↔ success)          | 0.38   |\n          | p-value                              | 0.003  |\n          | ACI convergence (episodes to ±0.02) | 148    |\n          | ACI achieved coverage               | 0.89   |\n          | Ellipsoid narrowing vs static        | 15.3%  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5a: Scatter plot of episode-level COC coverage fraction (x) vs task success (y, jittered binary). Spearman ρ=0.38 annotated. Clear positive trend but substantial scatter.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: ACI convergence plot — alpha_t over 300 episodes with target coverage line (0.90). Shows initial overshoot, oscillation, and eventual convergence. Shaded band = 5 seed variance.\"\n      - \"Fig 5c: Ellipsoid volume CDF — adaptive (solid) vs static (dashed) at matched 90% coverage. Adaptive curve shifted left, demonstrating 15.3% tighter volumes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC is validated as a positive but weak signal. ρ=0.38 is statistically significant (p=0.003)\n      but far from the \"reliable\" threshold that would make COC a standalone monitoring tool. The paper\n      should position COC as: \"a step toward fully self-referential uncertainty monitoring that shows\n      promise but requires further development for deployment-grade reliability.\" ACI convergence\n      demonstrates the theoretical guarantee holds in practice, but the slow convergence and overshoot\n      suggest that COC's noisy signal makes online adaptation sluggish. The ellipsoid narrowing result\n      is the strongest evidence for ACI's value — same coverage with tighter sets means more informative\n      uncertainty features for CRM.\n\n  - name: \"A1 Ablation — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      Full CRM with per-DOF conformal features, but CGS thresholds fixed at calibration-set values —\n      no online ACI adaptation. COC is not computed. Tests the marginal contribution of ACI.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 67.8\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 5.5\n      a1_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.7\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.4\n      static_coverage_at_early_episodes: 0.93\n      static_coverage_at_late_episodes: 0.82\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without ACI, conformal thresholds are calibrated on the training distribution and remain fixed.\n      Under LIBERO-Plus perturbations, the calibration set is no longer representative: early evaluation\n      episodes see conservative over-coverage (0.93 vs target 0.90) because perturbations haven't fully\n      diverged from calibration conditions, but later episodes see under-coverage (0.82) as distribution\n      shift accumulates. This miscalibration means CRM receives systematically biased uncertainty features —\n      over-estimated for some perturbation types, under-estimated for others. The 2.7% gap to full ACR-VLA\n      reflects that ACI's online correction produces better-calibrated inputs for CRM, particularly for\n      perturbation dimensions where the shift is gradual and ACI can track it (camera, lighting). For\n      abrupt shifts (layout, language), ACI has less time to adapt, so the gap is smaller there.\n      The result validates ACI's contribution but shows it's not the dominant factor — static conformal\n      features still carry most of the correction signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A1)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Full |\n          |-----------------------|-------------|-----------|\n          | A1: CRM, static CGS  | 67.8        | -2.7      |\n          | Full ACR-VLA          | 70.5        | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3 (ablation chart). Additionally: supplementary figure showing coverage drift over episodes — static (drifting down) vs ACI-adaptive (hovering near 0.90).\"\n    analysis: >\n      ACI contributes ~2.7% absolute, which is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features\n      themselves. The coverage drift analysis (0.93→0.82 for static) provides a clean mechanistic\n      explanation for why adaptive calibration helps. This supports Claim 2 but with caveats —\n      ACI is beneficial but not transformative.\n\n  - name: \"A2 Ablation — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + ACI pipeline but instead of CRM, uses multivariate-CP-guided sample selection\n      (extend ReconVLA's approach to leverage per-DOF conformal radii for selecting best sample\n      from K=10). No learned correction, just informed selection.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 66.9\n      libero_plus_gain_over_base: 4.6\n      a2_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 3.6\n      libero_nominal_success_rate: 94.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection with multivariate CP features is a strict improvement over ReconVLA's scalar\n      selection — per-DOF conformal radii enable selecting the sample that is best specifically in\n      the most-uncertain dimensions, rather than best in aggregate Euclidean distance. The 4.6% gain\n      over base is substantial and validates that CGS features contain useful information. However,\n      selection is fundamentally limited to choosing among the K=10 samples — it cannot synthesize\n      an action better than any individual sample, which is CRM's advantage. The 3.6% gap to full\n      ACR-VLA quantifies CRM's contribution as a learned synthesizer. Notably, the 0.6% nominal drop\n      is much smaller than full ACR-VLA's 2.2% — because sample selection is conservative (it only\n      picks from generated samples, never modifying them), it's less likely to degrade in-distribution\n      performance. This creates an interesting trade-off for the paper to discuss.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Table Row (A2)\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant               | LIBERO-Plus | LIBERO (nom.) | Δ Plus vs Full |\n          |----------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------|\n          | A2: Selection only   | 66.9        | 94.2          | -3.6           |\n          | Full ACR-VLA         | 70.5        | 92.6          | —              |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Part of Fig 3. A2 bar annotated with note: 'selection-only — lower OOD gain but minimal nominal drop.'\"\n    analysis: >\n      A2 provides a useful reference point — it validates that CGS features enable better sample\n      selection (extending ReconVLA), and quantifies CRM's value as a learned corrector (+3.6%).\n      The nominal preservation advantage of A2 is worth discussing as a practical deployment\n      consideration: if nominal drop must be minimized, selection-only is safer.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation-Dimension Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Detailed ACR-VLA gain analysis across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions, evaluated\n      per-task (10 tasks × 7 dimensions = 70 cells). Statistical significance via paired\n      bootstrap (1000 resamples, α=0.05) over 5 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      camera_shift_gain: 12.6\n      camera_shift_p_value: 0.001\n      sensor_noise_gain: 10.8\n      sensor_noise_p_value: 0.002\n      robot_state_gain: 8.5\n      robot_state_p_value: 0.008\n      lighting_gain: 5.8\n      lighting_p_value: 0.031\n      background_gain: 3.2\n      background_p_value: 0.112\n      layout_gain: 0.9\n      layout_p_value: 0.483\n      language_gain: 0.9\n      language_p_value: 0.501\n      dims_with_significant_gain: 4\n      dims_significant_list: \"camera_shift, sensor_noise, robot_state, lighting\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The per-perturbation structure directly reflects synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter,\n      Gaussian sensor noise, and robot-state perturbation are all covered by synthetic augmentation —\n      CGS sees similar distribution shifts during calibration, producing well-calibrated conformal features\n      that CRM can exploit. Camera shift shows the largest gain (+12.6%) because it primarily affects\n      translational DOFs (x, y) where per-DOF conformal features are most discriminative — camera shift\n      makes position uncertain while orientation remains relatively stable, and per-DOF features capture\n      this asymmetry. Lighting (+5.8%) reaches significance because brightness changes create predictable\n      patterns in flow-matching sample variance. Background changes (+3.2%) cause modest, diffuse\n      uncertainty increase that CRM partially addresses, but the effect is not statistically significant\n      at α=0.05. Layout (+0.9%) and language (+0.9%) are essentially unaddressed because these are\n      semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — the calibration set contains no examples\n      of rearranged furniture or paraphrased instructions, so CGS features under these perturbations\n      are poorly calibrated and CRM cannot extract useful correction signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gains\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation   | Base  | ACR-VLA | Δ     | p-value | Sig.? |\n          |---------------|-------|---------|-------|---------|-------|\n          | camera_shift  | 58.2  | 70.8    | +12.6 | 0.001   | ✓     |\n          | sensor_noise  | 55.6  | 66.4    | +10.8 | 0.002   | ✓     |\n          | robot_state   | 56.8  | 65.3    | +8.5  | 0.008   | ✓     |\n          | lighting      | 65.4  | 71.2    | +5.8  | 0.031   | ✓     |\n          | background    | 68.3  | 71.5    | +3.2  | 0.112   | ✗     |\n          | layout        | 72.1  | 73.0    | +0.9  | 0.483   | ✗     |\n          | language      | 71.5  | 72.4    | +0.9  | 0.501   | ✗     |\n      - name: \"Table 4b: Per-Task × Per-Perturbation Heatmap Data (top-5 tasks by gain)\"\n        content: |\n          | Task              | camera | noise | robot | light | bg   | layout | lang |\n          |-------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|------|--------|------|\n          | pick_place_can    | +18.0  | +14.0 | +12.0 | +8.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\n          | open_drawer       | +16.0  | +12.0 | +10.0 | +6.0  | +4.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\n          | stack_blocks      | +14.0  | +14.0 | +10.0 | +8.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +0.0 |\n          | close_microwave   | +12.0  | +10.0 | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\n          | turn_faucet       | +10.0  | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Radar chart showing ACR-VLA gain (%) across 7 perturbation dimensions. Clear asymmetry — gain concentrated in camera/noise/robot-state lobe, flat on layout/language.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: 10×7 heatmap of per-task per-perturbation success rate deltas. Reveals task-perturbation interaction patterns.\"\n      - \"Fig 6c: Correlation between synthetic augmentation coverage (measured as calibration set overlap with each perturbation) and ACR-VLA gain. Strong positive correlation (r=0.91).\"\n    analysis: >\n      4/7 dimensions with significant gain is within [3, 5]. This result is central to the median-tier\n      narrative: ACR-VLA works well where calibration coverage exists, honestly fails where it doesn't.\n      The paper should present this transparency as a strength — \"we clearly identify the boundary of\n      our method's applicability and provide a mechanistic explanation.\" The correlation between\n      augmentation coverage and gain (r=0.91) is a strong explanatory result that reviewers will\n      appreciate. Layout/language as honest limitations, with ACI online adaptation as partial mitigation\n      and future work on semantic augmentation as natural extension.\n\n  - name: \"A5 Ablation — CGS Joint Mahalanobis vs Per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare full CGS (joint Mahalanobis with Ledoit-Wolf shrunk covariance) vs per-DOF independent\n      Bonferroni-corrected intervals. Tests whether multivariate CP's joint structure provides value\n      beyond simply having per-DOF information.\n    expected_results:\n      joint_mahalanobis_libero_plus: 70.5\n      bonferroni_libero_plus: 69.1\n      gap_joint_vs_bonferroni: 1.4\n      bonferroni_coverage: 0.94\n      joint_coverage: 0.90\n      bonferroni_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 1.0  # normalized\n      joint_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 0.72\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bonferroni correction treats each DOF independently and inflates thresholds to maintain joint\n      coverage — producing conservative, hypercubic confidence regions that ignore cross-DOF covariance.\n      Joint Mahalanobis with shrunk covariance produces ellipsoidal regions aligned with the actual\n      covariance structure, yielding tighter (28% smaller volume) regions at the same coverage level.\n      The 1.4% performance gap comes from CRM receiving more informative features: joint CGS features\n      encode which DOF pairs co-vary (e.g., x and y under camera shift), enabling CRM to make correlated\n      corrections across related DOFs. With Bonferroni, CRM sees each DOF's uncertainty independently\n      and must re-learn any cross-DOF correction patterns from the observation embedding alone. The gap\n      is modest because K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is noisy — the theoretical advantage of joint\n      Mahalanobis is attenuated by covariance estimation error, and Bonferroni's conservative margins\n      partially compensate by providing wider \"safety padding.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A5 Ablation Results\"\n        content: |\n          | CGS Variant         | LIBERO-Plus | Coverage | Avg. Volume |\n          |--------------------|-------------|----------|-------------|\n          | Joint Mahalanobis  | 70.5        | 0.90     | 0.72 (norm) |\n          | Per-DOF Bonferroni | 69.1        | 0.94     | 1.00 (norm) |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S1: 2D slices of confidence regions — ellipsoidal (joint) vs rectangular (Bonferroni) for example action dimensions. Shows tighter ellipsoidal regions capturing covariance tilt.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.4% gap is small but validates that joint multivariate CP provides value through\n      covariance-aware regions. Worth reporting as a supplementary ablation to support the\n      multivariate CP design choice. The over-coverage of Bonferroni (0.94 vs target 0.90) is\n      an inherent inefficiency that the joint approach avoids.\n\n  - name: \"A6 Ablation — COC Signal vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare ACI driven by COC (self-referential, per-step) vs ACI driven by episode-level binary\n      task success (stronger signal, but requires ground-truth outcome and only updates once per episode).\n    expected_results:\n      coc_based_aci_libero_plus: 70.5\n      episode_based_aci_libero_plus: 71.8\n      gap_episode_vs_coc: 1.3\n      coc_aci_convergence_episodes: 148\n      episode_aci_convergence_episodes: 42\n      coc_aci_coverage_variance: 0.018\n      episode_aci_coverage_variance: 0.006\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level success is a stronger, lower-variance signal for ACI — it directly reflects whether\n      the uncertainty calibration led to good outcomes. COC is noisy because chunk overlap consistency\n      is only a proxy for prediction quality, and individual step-level COC values have high variance.\n      The 1.3% gap reflects that episode-based ACI converges ~3.5x faster (42 vs 148 episodes) and\n      maintains tighter coverage tracking (variance 0.006 vs 0.018). However, episode-level success\n      requires ground-truth task outcomes — available in simulation but not in real deployment. This\n      is the key argument for COC: it's self-referential (no external supervision). The paper should\n      present this as a trade-off: \"episode-level ACI is preferable when outcomes are available;\n      COC-based ACI sacrifices ~1.3% performance for full self-referentiality.\" In the median tier,\n      COC's disadvantage is noticeable but not disqualifying — it still converges and provides meaningful\n      adaptation, just more slowly and with more variance.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A6 Ablation: ACI Signal Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | ACI Signal     | LIBERO-Plus | Convergence (eps) | Coverage Var. |\n          |---------------|-------------|-------------------|---------------|\n          | COC (self-ref)| 70.5        | 148               | 0.018         |\n          | Episode success| 71.8       | 42                | 0.006         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S2: Side-by-side ACI convergence plots — COC-driven (noisy, slow) vs episode-driven (smooth, fast). Both converge to target coverage but with very different dynamics.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Episode-based ACI is clearly superior when ground-truth is available. COC's value proposition\n      is self-referentiality for deployment scenarios without outcome feedback. The 1.3% gap and 3.5x\n      convergence slowdown are honest costs of self-referentiality. Paper recommendation: use episode-level\n      ACI as the default in simulation experiments; present COC as a deployment-oriented variant with\n      known performance trade-offs. This supports repositioning COC as \"promising future work\" rather\n      than the primary ACI signal.\n\n  - name: \"A7 Ablation — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replace CGS epistemic uncertainty features (45D) with DiU-style aleatoric uncertainty features\n      (7D per-DOF variance from single forward pass). Also test combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D).\n      Validates that epistemic (distribution-shift-sensitive) features are more useful than aleatoric\n      (in-distribution-also-high) features for OOD correction.\n    expected_results:\n      cgs_epistemic_libero_plus: 70.5\n      diu_aleatoric_libero_plus: 67.4\n      combined_52d_libero_plus: 71.1\n      gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric: 3.1\n      cgs_epistemic_libero_nominal: 92.6\n      diu_aleatoric_libero_nominal: 93.9\n      combined_52d_libero_nominal: 92.4\n      diu_aleatoric_in_dist_variance: 0.048\n      cgs_epistemic_in_dist_variance: 0.012\n      diu_aleatoric_ood_variance: 0.063\n      cgs_epistemic_ood_variance: 0.089\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU captures aleatoric uncertainty — inherent action ambiguity that exists even in-distribution.\n      Under distribution shift, DiU's signal increases only modestly (+31%, 0.048→0.063) because most of\n      what DiU measures is irreducible noise. CGS epistemic features, in contrast, are low in-distribution\n      (0.012) and spike dramatically under shift (+642%, 0.012→0.089), providing a clear signal for \"this\n      is out-of-distribution.\" CRM conditioned on epistemic features can discriminate OOD steps that need\n      correction from in-distribution steps that don't. With aleatoric features, CRM sees consistently\n      elevated uncertainty everywhere, reducing its ability to be selective. The 3.1% gap validates the\n      epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction. The combined 52D features provide a marginal +0.6% over epistemic\n      alone — aleatoric features add some complementary information about inherent action ambiguity (useful\n      for multi-modal tasks like placing objects with rotational symmetry), but the gain is small because\n      most of the relevant signal is already captured by epistemic features. The lower nominal drop of DiU\n      (0.9% vs 2.2% for CGS) reflects that aleatoric features are less \"aggressive\" — they don't provoke\n      large corrections because their signal doesn't differentiate ID from OOD.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"A7 Ablation: Feature Type Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Type        | Dim | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus vs Base |\n          |--------------------|-----|---------------|-------------|----------------|\n          | CGS epistemic       | 45  | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2           |\n          | DiU aleatoric       | 7   | 93.9          | 67.4        | +5.1           |\n          | Combined            | 52  | 92.4          | 71.1        | +8.8           |\n      - name: \"A7: Signal Discrimination\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Source | In-Dist Var. | OOD Var. | Ratio (OOD/ID) |\n          |--------------|-------------|---------|----------------|\n          | CGS epistemic | 0.012       | 0.089   | 7.42           |\n          | DiU aleatoric | 0.048       | 0.063   | 1.31           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S3a: Violin plots of feature magnitude distributions (ID vs OOD) for epistemic and aleatoric features. CGS shows clear bimodal separation; DiU shows overlapping distributions.\"\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S3b: CRM correction magnitude conditioned on feature type — epistemic-conditioned CRM applies larger corrections OOD and smaller ID; aleatoric-conditioned CRM applies similar corrections everywhere.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 7.42x OOD/ID ratio for epistemic features vs 1.31x for aleatoric validates the core design\n      choice. This is one of the cleaner results — it mechanistically explains why flow-matching sample\n      diversity (epistemic) is more informative than single-pass variance (aleatoric) for distribution\n      shift detection. The combined 52D result (+0.6% over epistemic alone) suggests that adding aleatoric\n      features is marginally beneficial — worth mentioning but not worth the complexity for most applications.\n\n  - name: \"Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient-based feature importance: compute |∂correction/∂feature| averaged over evaluation episodes,\n      broken down by perturbation type. Identifies which of the 45 CGS features drive CRM's corrections\n      for each perturbation dimension.\n    expected_results:\n      top_features_camera_shift: \"per-DOF std (x, y) — 34% of total gradient; conformal radius (x, y) — 22%\"\n      top_features_sensor_noise: \"per-DOF std (all DOFs) — 41%; ellipsoid volume — 18%\"\n      top_features_robot_state: \"conformal radius (joint 1-4) — 38%; per-DOF std (joint 1-4) — 25%\"\n      top_features_lighting: \"per-DOF std (x, y, z) — 29%; ellipsoid volume — 21%\"\n      per_dof_std_importance_share: 0.42\n      conformal_radius_importance_share: 0.31\n      ellipsoid_volume_importance_share: 0.14\n      cross_dof_covariance_importance_share: 0.13\n    result_rationale: >\n      Per-DOF std features are the most important (42% of gradient magnitude) because they directly encode\n      \"how uncertain is this specific DOF\" — the most actionable information for dimension-selective\n      correction. Conformal radius features rank second (31%) because they encode calibrated confidence\n      intervals that are more robust than raw std to distributional assumptions. Ellipsoid volume (14%) is\n      a global uncertainty summary — useful for CRM's gating mechanism (how much total correction to apply)\n      but less informative for dimension selection. Cross-DOF covariance features (13%) capture correlation\n      structure; their moderate importance reflects that K=10 covariance estimates are noisy, limiting\n      the information content of off-diagonal terms. The perturbation-specific patterns are illuminating:\n      camera shift activates translational DOF features (x, y) because it primarily affects workspace\n      position estimation; robot-state perturbation activates joint-specific conformal radii because it\n      directly perturbs joint configurations; sensor noise activates broad std features because it\n      diffusely increases all DOF uncertainties.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Feature Importance by Category\"\n        content: |\n          | Feature Category    | Importance Share | Primary Perturbations |\n          |--------------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n          | Per-DOF std (7D)   | 42%             | noise, camera, light |\n          | Conformal radius (7D)| 31%           | robot-state, camera  |\n          | Ellipsoid volume (1D)| 14%           | all (gating)         |\n          | Cross-DOF cov (30D) | 13%            | robot-state          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Stacked bar chart — feature importance share by category, one bar per perturbation dimension. Shows distinct feature activation patterns per perturbation type.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: 45-feature × 7-perturbation heatmap of gradient magnitudes. Reveals sparse, interpretable activation patterns — CRM learns perturbation-specific feature usage without explicit supervision.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The feature importance analysis validates the dimension-selective correction mechanism: CRM\n      learns to attend to different uncertainty features for different perturbation types, without\n      explicit supervision about which perturbation is active. This is a compelling interpretability\n      result for the paper. The 42%+31% = 73% importance of per-DOF features (std + conformal radius)\n      supports Claim 1's emphasis on per-DOF structure, even though the A4 ablation showed only +2.1%\n      over scalar — the mechanism is clearly there, even if the aggregate performance gain is modest.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace K=10 full denoising trajectories with K=10 single-step distilled generation via\n      SnapFlow-style consistency distillation. Test: (a) inference speedup, (b) sample diversity\n      preservation, (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance.\n    expected_results:\n      full_denoising_inference_ms: 118\n      snapflow_1step_inference_ms: 38\n      speedup_factor: 3.1\n      diversity_trace_ratio: 0.83\n      full_denoising_libero_plus: 70.5\n      snapflow_libero_plus: 69.8\n      performance_drop: 0.7\n      snapflow_covariance_frobenius_error: 0.21\n    result_rationale: >\n      Single-step distilled generation produces samples ~3.1x faster but with reduced diversity:\n      the trace ratio of 0.83 means 1-step samples capture 83% of the covariance structure of\n      full denoising samples. The 17% diversity loss comes from the distillation process collapsing\n      minor modes in the action distribution — the dominant mode is well-preserved but tail samples\n      are pulled inward. This propagates to a modest 0.7% ACR-VLA performance drop because the\n      CGS features computed from less-diverse samples have slightly attenuated covariance structure,\n      reducing the per-DOF signal quality. The Frobenius error of 0.21 (normalized) in covariance\n      estimation means the Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage target absorbs more weight, further compressing the\n      estimated covariance toward the identity-like shrinkage target. The practical trade-off is\n      favorable: 3.1x speedup for 0.7% accuracy cost, making real-time deployment more feasible.\n      This is a supplementary result that strengthens the practical narrative without affecting claims.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        content: |\n          | Sampling Method     | K=10 Time (ms) | Diversity | LIBERO-Plus | Δ   |\n          |--------------------|----------------|-----------|-------------|-----|\n          | Full denoising     | 118            | 1.00      | 70.5        | —   |\n          | SnapFlow 1-step    | 38             | 0.83      | 69.8        | -0.7|\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig S4: Pareto plot — inference time vs LIBERO-Plus success rate. SnapFlow point near the Pareto frontier, showing favorable speed-accuracy trade-off.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration is a practical engineering contribution. The 0.7% drop is small enough\n      that the 3.1x speedup is clearly worthwhile for deployment. The diversity reduction is the\n      primary concern — if future work increases K beyond 10, SnapFlow's diversity loss may compound.\n      Report as supplementary result demonstrating deployment feasibility.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"ef3a4a7f-e7d7-4354-91e3-42aa53e20677","total_cost_usd":0.548748,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11182,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":18667,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11182,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18667,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11182,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1